Since H − is invariant under permutation, V ( c ) i 1 = c , and V ( c ) i = c 1 . For case 2, exactly the same argument as the above paragraph with the same h ′′ and h ′′′ shows that the coordinates of V ( p ) i is ± c . Therefore, we have shown that for any i ∈ I − , V ( c ) i = c 1 for same c> 0 . The symmetry of distribution immediately tells us for any i ∈ I + , V ( c ) i = c 1 for c< 0 . On the downstream distribution P ∗ , since there is no masked token, only V ( c ) i is working. Since V ( c ) i = c 1 always holds, we complete the proof. 21 Lemma D.5. Suppose M ∈ R (2 k +1) × (2 k +1) is a matrix composed of ± 1 . The first row of M is [1 ,..., 1 (cid:124) (cid:123)(cid:122) (cid:125) k+11’s , − 1 ,..., − 1 (cid:124) (cid:123)(cid:122) (cid:125) k-1’s ] . Define a permutation ρ (1)=2 , ρ (2)=3 ... ρ (2 k +1)=1 . For all i ≥ 2 , M i,ρ ( j ) = M i − 1 ,j . Then the rank of M is 2 k +1 . Proof of Lemma D.5. Note that M i + M ρ k +1 ( i ) =2 e i for all i ∈ [2 k +1] , which means we can express the orthonormal basis as linear combination of the rows in M . Therefore, the rank of M is 2 k +1 . 22
